  <section id="breakthrough" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">The breakthrough: stop watching metrics, diff the checkpoints</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">If the model trains fine but some saved checkpoints are broken, then <i>something must be physically different</i> about those checkpoints. The idea that finally cracked it (from the same AI agent, after the eval-code dead end) was almost too blunt to take seriously: stop staring at curves and just <b>diff the weights</b>. For every tensor in the model, measure how much it changed from a good checkpoint to the next, bad one — relative to its own size, <code>‖ΔW‖ / ‖W‖</code> — and sort.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">One entry detonated off the chart — its relative change was about <b>3,000× larger</b> than the next tensor's (a five-order-of-magnitude span over the run). And it wasn't a weight at all: it was <code>first_conv.running_var</code>, a <b>buffer</b> inside the first BatchNorm layer — something gradient descent, and therefore the loss, never touches.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">That single line is the whole bug. The minimal reproduction from the opening lets us watch it happen safely: diff two of <i>its</i> adjacent checkpoints tensor-by-tensor and the same fingerprint appears — one buffer towers over every learnable parameter:</p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
