      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. 3 — Relative change per tensor between a good checkpoint and the next, bad one. One BatchNorm buffer dwarfs every learnable weight.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Same story in miniature: every learnable parameter nudges by a fraction of a percent, while one BatchNorm buffer moves by hundreds of times its own size in a single checkpoint interval — over <b>1,700× more</b> than the next-largest tensor. The rest of this post is what that buffer is, why a few training samples send it flying, and how to fix it.</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Now the plain strip from the background pays off. Same encoder, second look — this time with the suspect marked. The exploding tensor lives in the <b>very first normalization layer</b>: the first place where raw coordinate magnitudes meet a statistic shared across samples.</p>
