  <section id="hidden-buffer" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">The hidden buffer</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1502.03167" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-700 hover:underline">BatchNorm</a> does two different things depending on the mode. During <b>training</b> it standardizes each feature using the mean and variance of <i>the current mini-batch</i>. During <b>evaluation</b> it can't depend on whatever other examples happen to share the batch, so it instead uses a fixed pair of buffers — <code>running_mean</code> and <code>running_var</code> — that it accumulated during training as an exponential moving average:</p>
    <div class="bg-white rounded border p-4 mb-4 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed">
      <b>train step:</b> <code>x̂ = (x − μ<sub>batch</sub>) / √(σ²<sub>batch</sub> + ε)</code><br/>
      <b>buffer update:</b> <code>running_var ← (1−m)·running_var + m·σ²<sub>batch</sub></code><br/>
      <b>eval:</b> <code>x̂ = (x − running_mean) / √(running_var + ε)</code>
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-amber-50 text-amber-900">
      <b>The crucial detail:</b> that buffer update is a plain moving average of batch statistics — it is <b>not driven by the gradient</b>. No loss term pushes on it. So the optimizer, the loss curve, and gradient-norm dashboards are all completely blind to it. One pathological batch can shove <code>running_var</code> orders of magnitude off, and nothing in training "notices."
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why-outliers" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Why a few outliers poison it</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">My point clouds are normalized by a <b>single dataset-wide divisor</b> rather than per-sample. That's deliberate: I want a 3&nbsp;mm part and a 4&nbsp;mm part to stay <i>different</i> after normalization, because absolute size is a real, discriminative feature. The cost is that there's <b>no per-sample clamp</b>: an object that is genuinely enormous (a building uploaded in millimeters, or a unit-error model) keeps enormous coordinates after "normalization".</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">The first convolution turns large coordinates into large activations, and variance grows with the <i>square</i> of the spread. The left panel below shows the distribution of per-sample extent (how far the coordinates reach after normalization) on a <b>log₁₀</b> axis: a healthy bulk at log₁₀ ≈ 0 (extent ≈ 1), with a detached tail of outliers out near log₁₀ ≈ 3–4 — that is, extent in the <b>thousands to ten-thousands</b>. The right panel plots the resulting first-layer variance against the raw extent (both axes log-scaled) — a clean <code>variance ∝ extent²</code> line, with the tail samples sitting at variances five to eight orders of magnitude above the bulk.</p>
    <figure class="bg-white rounded border p-2 mb-2">
      <img src="/plots/batchnorm-poisoning/data_diagnostic.png" alt="Left: histogram of per-sample extent with a long outlier tail. Right: first-conv variance grows as extent squared." class="w-full rounded" />
      <figcaption class="text-sm text-gray-500 px-2 pt-2">Fig. 5 — The driver: a detached tail of extreme-extent samples (left) whose first-layer variance grows as extent² (right).</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">When one of those tail samples lands in a batch, the batch variance is dominated by it, the moving average drinks it in, and <code>running_var</code> spikes. At the next eval, every clean input gets divided by that inflated variance, the embedding collapses, and retrieval falls to chance — until enough clean batches pass for the moving average to recover. That's the see-saw.</p>

    <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold mt-8 mb-2">The update, straight from the source</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Is the buffer updated per <i>sample</i> or per <i>batch</i>? Don't take my word for it — this is PyTorch's own reference implementation of the BatchNorm kernel (trimmed from <code>torch/_decomp</code>, torch 2.9):</p>
    <div class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded border p-4 mb-3 text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto font-mono">
      <span class="text-gray-400"># stats are pooled over the BATCH dim and all points — per channel</span><br/>
      reduction_dims = [0] + list(range(2, input.dim()))<br/>
      biased_var, mean = torch.var_mean(input, dim=reduction_dims, correction=0)<br/>
      <br/>
      n = input.numel() / input.shape[1]&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-400"># elements pooled per channel = batch × points</span><br/>
      unbiased_var = biased_var * (n / (n - 1))<br/>
      new_running_var = momentum * unbiased_var + (1 - momentum) * running_var<br/>
      running_var.copy_(new_running_var)&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-400"># executed ONCE per forward call</span>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">So: <b>per batch, per channel — never per sample</b>. A sample doesn't get its own update it could be outvoted on; instead every sample and every point in the batch is pooled into <i>one</i> variance number, and a single giant sample dominates that number for everyone. (Two details worth noticing: normalization uses the <i>biased</i> variance, the buffer stores the <i>unbiased</i> one — and the update runs on every train-mode forward whether or not you ever call the optimizer.)</p>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">Strip the tensor machinery away and the buffer's whole life is a fold over a list of batch variances:</p>
    <div class="bg-gray-900 text-gray-100 rounded border p-4 mb-3 text-sm md:text-base leading-relaxed overflow-x-auto font-mono">
      def running_var_after(batch_vars, m=0.1, rv=1.0):<br/>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;for sigma2 in batch_vars:&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-400"># one EMA step per BATCH</span><br/>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;rv = (1 - m) * rv + m * sigma2<br/>
      &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;return rv<br/>
      <br/>
      <span class="text-gray-400"># our scenario, as a list:</span><br/>
      running_var_after([0.006] * 200)&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-400"># → 0.006&nbsp;&nbsp;(healthy)</span><br/>
      running_var_after([0.006] * 200 + [343])&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-400"># → 34.3&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(one poisoned batch: ×5,700)</span><br/>
      running_var_after([0.006] * 200 + [343] + [0.006] * 22)&nbsp;&nbsp;<span class="text-gray-400"># → 3.4 — still ×570, 22 batches later</span>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">The recovery is the slow part: each clean batch only multiplies the damage by <code>(1−m) = 0.9</code>, so getting from ×5,700 back under ×10 takes <code>log(570)/log(1/0.9) ≈ 60</code> clean batches. Whether a checkpoint lands inside that window is pure timing — that's the lottery.</p>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-blue-50 text-blue-900 mb-3">
      <b>Try it yourself.</b> The <a href="/demos/bn-poisoning" class="underline font-semibold">live simulator</a> runs exactly this update with our scenario's numbers pre-loaded — momentum, batch size, outlier size and frequency are all sliders. Inject an outlier with a button and watch the buffer spike and crawl back.
    </div>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-amber-50 text-amber-900">
      <b>But if the outlier is in a training batch, why doesn't the <i>loss</i> spike too?</b> Two mechanisms conspire — and this is worth verifying with actual numbers, because neither alone would be enough. <b>First, the poisoned micro-batch's loss is capped.</b> In train mode BatchNorm normalizes the batch by its own (huge) variance, and the embeddings are L2-normalized before the contrastive loss — so the worst a collapsed micro-batch can score is roughly <i>chance level</i>, <code>ln 36 ≈ 3.6</code>, not something astronomical. <b>Second, real training averages over a large effective batch</b> — gradient accumulation over many micro-batches, usually across several GPUs — so that already-capped bump is further divided by the accumulation factor. BatchNorm's buffer gets neither protection: statistics are computed <b>per micro-batch</b> and absorbed into <code>running_var</code> at full strength. <b>The loss path is capped then diluted; the buffer path is neither.</b>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-700 mt-4 mb-2">Here are the receipts — one dissected optimizer step of the reproduction (micro-batch 36, accumulation 12), the step where an outlier landed:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-2">
      <table class="w-full text-sm md:text-base border-collapse">
        <thead><tr class="border-b text-left">
          <th class="py-2 pr-4">signal</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">typical step</th><th class="py-2 pr-4">the outlier step</th><th class="py-2">change</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody class="text-gray-700">
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">outlier micro-batch loss</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">~1.25</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">3.73</td><td class="py-2">capped at the chance ceiling (<code>ln 36 = 3.58</code>)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">logged loss (mean of 12 micro-batches)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1.23 (median)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1.46</td><td class="py-2">+18% — <b>below</b> the run's own clean-step noise (max 1.68)</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4">gradient norm</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1.22 (median)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">1.54</td><td class="py-2">×1.3 — a shrug</td></tr>
          <tr class="border-b"><td class="py-2 pr-4"><code>running_var</code></td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0.031</td><td class="py-2 pr-4"><b>34.3</b></td><td class="py-2 text-rose-700"><b>×1,100 in one update</b> (0.9·0.031 + 0.1·343)</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="py-2 pr-4">eval MRR at the next checkpoint</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">~0.35 (healthy)</td><td class="py-2 pr-4">0.024</td><td class="py-2 text-rose-700">chance level — collapsed</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3">The window arithmetic is the whole story in one line: <code>(11 × 1.25 + 3.73) / 12 = 1.46</code> — a blip inside the noise — while the buffer takes the outlier's variance at full strength: <code>0.9 × 0.031 + 0.1 × 343 = 34.3</code>. One signal moves 18%; the other moves three orders of magnitude. <span class="text-gray-500">(Reproduce: <code>dissect_outlier_step.py</code> in the repo.)</span></p>
