    <p class="text-gray-700 mb-3"><b>Why it's a fix.</b> It <b>decouples shape from scale</b>: geometry stays scale-invariant and robust, while scale enters through a separate, controllable path rather than an accidental leak. The result (<code>unitbb + GN + FiLM</code>, green) is the best of both worlds — rock-stable <i>and</i> the strongest size discrimination of any variant (0.91 vs 0.00), with the best retrieval too.</p>
    <div class="p-4 rounded border bg-rose-50 text-rose-900 mb-3">
      <b>Still not perfect.</b> The model can only learn the size sense its teacher rewards, and that geometry turns out to be <i>categorical, not metric</i> — a size-16 part is about as far from size-18 as from size-64 — so 0.91 is discrimination against a teacher that isn't itself a true "how many mm" sense. And a single log-size is a blunt summary for a long, thin part: FiLM applies one global modulation, not per-region.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="takeaways" class="mt-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold mb-3">Takeaways</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-700 space-y-1">
      <li>BatchNorm carries hidden state (<code>running_var</code>/<code>running_mean</code>) that the loss never touches — so a healthy loss curve does <b>not</b> guarantee a healthy eval.</li>
      <li>If your eval score jumps between adjacent checkpoints for no visible reason, log the BN running buffers. An anti-correlated buffer is the smoking gun.</li>
      <li>A single-divisor normalization with no per-sample clamp lets scale outliers through, and variance grows as extent² — so the tail does the damage.</li>
      <li>GroupNorm removes the instability for free, but it (and every per-sample norm) is scale-invariant — if absolute scale matters to you, you have to inject it back deliberately.</li>
      <li>FiLM conditioning on a log-scale Fourier embedding is a clean way to do that: shape stays scale-invariant and stable; scale becomes its own bounded, explicit signal.</li>
      <li>None of these fixes clean the <i>data</i>. Genuine unit-error uploads (a house modeled in kilometers) still belong filtered upstream — separate from <i>legitimately</i> huge parts, which unit-box and FiLM handle fine. "Fix the norm layer" and "fix the dataset" are different jobs.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

